WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.647
Axum gives Rust teams a practical way to
build web APIs, internal services,

2
00:00:07.727 --> 00:00:12.265
backend applications, and operational
tools.

3
00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:18.924
It fits naturally with the Rust ecosystem
by using routes, handlers,

4
00:00:19.004 --> 00:00:27.059
extractors, shared state, middleware,
Tower layers, and asynchronous execution.

5
00:00:27.139 --> 00:00:30.315
Those pieces make it productive,

6
00:00:30.395 --> 00:00:38.223
but they also create the security boundary
where untrusted requests meet trusted

7
00:00:38.303 --> 00:00:40.571
application behavior.

8
00:00:40.651 --> 00:00:48.819
Rust's memory-safety model removes many
classes of low-level implementation risk,

9
00:00:48.899 --> 00:00:54.912
but it does not automatically solve web
application security.

10
00:00:54.992 --> 00:00:59.870
An Axum service can still authorize the
wrong user,

11
00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:07.664
accept input that is syntactically valid
but unsafe for the business operation,

12
00:01:07.744 --> 00:01:14.210
leak internal details in an error
response, apply CORS too broadly,

13
00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:21.096
log sensitive tokens, or accept enough
work to exhaust a database pool.

14
00:01:21.176 --> 00:01:28.096
The important shift is to treat an Axum
application as a boundary system.

15
00:01:28.176 --> 00:01:32.033
Routes define what outsiders can reach.

16
00:01:32.113 --> 00:01:36.538
Handlers decide how requests become
actions.

17
00:01:36.618 --> 00:01:42.063
Extractors shape how untrusted input
enters typed code.

18
00:01:42.143 --> 00:01:46.567
Middleware applies cross-cutting behavior.

19
00:01:46.647 --> 00:01:51.866
Shared state exposes clients, pools,
configuration,

20
00:01:51.946 --> 00:01:56.143
and service handles to request processing.

21
00:01:56.223 --> 00:02:03.029
Each of those areas deserves design
review, not just compiler success.

22
00:02:03.109 --> 00:02:08.441
Secure Axum development is not about
distrusting Rust.

23
00:02:08.521 --> 00:02:16.235
It is about being precise about what Rust
protects and what the application still

24
00:02:16.315 --> 00:02:16.883
owns.

25
00:02:16.963 --> 00:02:24.336
The compiler helps with ownership,
lifetimes, and many concurrency issues.

26
00:02:24.416 --> 00:02:31.336
The engineering team still owns identity,
authorization, validation,

27
00:02:31.416 --> 00:02:38.450
error mapping, request limits, dependency
governance, configuration,

28
00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:43.521
logging, monitoring, and deployment
assumptions.

29
00:02:43.601 --> 00:02:49.273
The defensive goal is a service that is
easy to reason about.

30
00:02:49.353 --> 00:02:52.303
Boundaries should be visible.

31
00:02:52.383 --> 00:02:55.673
Defaults should be conservative.

32
00:02:55.753 --> 00:02:58.475
Privilege should be narrow.

33
00:02:58.555 --> 00:03:00.937
Failures should be safe.

34
00:03:01.017 --> 00:03:06.690
Logs should support operations without
exposing secrets.

35
00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:10.173
When a reviewer opens the codebase,

36
00:03:10.253 --> 00:03:15.812
they should be able to understand who can
reach each route,

37
00:03:15.892 --> 00:03:20.996
what input is trusted, where authorization
happens,

38
00:03:21.076 --> 00:03:24.820
and how risky behavior is constrained.
